cause-archive-config-not-a-digest = { $observed } is not an image config digest
cause-archive-index-lists-no-manifests = { $entry } lists no manifests
cause-archive-index-digest-unusable = { $entry } names a manifest by { $observed }, which is not a digest
cause-archive-tag-not-a-string = a repository tag is not a string
cause-archive-holds-another-image = the archive holds { $observed }, not { $declared }
cause-archive-label-differs = the image in the archive declares { $key }: { $observed }, expected { $expected }
cause-archive-entry-has-no-config = { $entry } has no image configuration
cause-archive-labels-not-an-object = { $entry } declares labels that are not an object
cause-archive-label-not-a-string = label { $key } in { $entry } is not a string
cause-archive-entry-unnamed = an entry has no readable name
cause-archive-entry-size-unreadable = entry { $entry } has no readable size
cause-archive-entry-too-large = { $entry } is { $observed } bytes, which is larger than sbxm reads
cause-git-directory-outside = it points at { $observed }, which is outside { $root }
cause-provider-unsupported = { $observed } is not a supported provider
cause-clone-transport-unsupported = { $observed } is not a supported clone transport
cause-clone-url-unrecognized = { $observed } is not one of { $accepted }
cause-clone-url-transport-mismatch = the clone URL uses the { $observed } transport, not { $declared }
cause-clone-url-identity-mismatch = the clone URL folds to { $observed }, not { $declared }
cause-clone-url-name-mismatch = the clone URL names { $observed }, not { $declared }
cause-local-repository-path-unrecognized = { $observed } is not a normalized absolute path
cause-local-project-name-unrecognized = { $observed } does not name a project
cause-not-writable = the directory is not writable by the current user
cause-value-empty = the value is empty
cause-value-has-line-break = the value contains a line break
cause-language-unsupported = { $value } is not one of { $supported }
external-output-heading = Output of { $program }:

error-sbx-version-unparseable = The Docker Sandboxes CLI version could not be determined from { $observed }.
error-sbx-version-below-minimum = Docker Sandboxes CLI { $observed } is older than the required { $minimum }.
error-platform-unsupported = This build supports { $expected }. Observed: { $observed }
error-platform-unobservable = The platform could not be determined
error-host-command-missing = The command { $program } was not found on PATH.
error-docker-unreachable = The Docker daemon did not answer
error-network-policy-mismatch = The network policy is { $observed }, but this build is validated only for { $expected }.
error-network-policy-unobservable = The network policy could not be read
error-daemon-unobservable = The Docker Sandboxes daemon state could not be read
error-sbx-login-missing = This host is not signed in to Docker Sandboxes.
error-sbx-login-unobservable = Whether this host is signed in to Docker Sandboxes could not be read
error-remote-ssh-unconfigured = ssh has no proxy configuration for { $host }, so sbxm cannot reach a sandbox over SSH.
error-remote-ssh-unobservable = The SSH configuration for sandboxes could not be read
remediation-run-help = See the arguments this command accepts.
remediation-host-clone-unusable = Inspect { $path } yourself, then move it aside or fix its origin before running the command again.
remediation-local-repository-unusable = Pass the Git directory of a repository on this host: the .git of a working tree, or a bare repository.
remediation-local-name-unusable = Choose a project name made of letters, digits, '.', '-', and '_', and pass it with --name.
remediation-host-repository-detached = Check out a branch in the host repository, or pass --detach with the branch to start from.
remediation-declared-file-conflict = Compare the two yourself, then replace what the sandbox holds with the declared file.
remediation-declared-file-transfer-incomplete = Check that this version of Docker Sandboxes passes standard input to a command run inside a sandbox, then run the command again.
remediation-sandbox-repository-over-ssh = Check that this host can connect to the sandbox over SSH.
remediation-sandbox-origin-refused = Look inside the sandbox for what refused the update, such as a hook in its repository, then run the command again.
remediation-host-repository-empty = Commit at least once in the host repository, then run the command again.
remediation-sync-requires-local = Open the sandbox and use Git with GitHub there instead.
remediation-project-inside-repository = Run the command from a directory outside the repository, such as the one that holds it.
remediation-host-repository-missing = Put the repository back at that path, then run the command again. If it has moved for good, remove this project and add the repository again from its new place.
remediation-declared-file-overwrite = Save anything you need from the sandbox copy, then run the command again with --force to replace it.
remediation-sandbox-identity-mismatch = Check whose sandbox this is. sbxm does not overwrite a value that is already set.
github-token-scopes = Issue a personal access token that can read and write this repository. A fine-grained token needs Contents read and write plus Metadata read; a classic token needs the repo scope. The token stays with the proxy and never enters the sandbox.
remediation-github-secret-missing = { github-token-scopes } Register it, then run the same command again.
remediation-github-secret-ambiguous = Several registrations cover these hosts for this sandbox, and nothing says which token the sandbox should present. Remove the ones that do not belong to this project, leaving one, then run the same command again.
remediation-github-credential-rejected = { github-token-scopes } Check that the token is still valid and still reaches this repository. Update the existing custom secret with the command below, keeping its placeholder, then run the same command again. The sandbox does not need to be recreated.
remediation-github-secret-incomplete = { github-token-scopes } A secret for this environment variable is already there, and registering it again without naming its placeholder is refused as a duplicate. Registering it this way keeps the placeholder the sandbox already holds. Then run the same command again.
remediation-secret-still-registered = A registration that stays behind makes the next registration for this project refused as a duplicate. Remove it, then run the same command again.
remediation-sandbox-repository-unusable = Inspect { $path } inside the sandbox yourself. sbxm never deletes a repository or a worktree to make room.
remediation-start-ref-unresolved = Check the branch name on GitHub, then run the command again once the branch exists.
remediation-project-incomplete = Run the registration again to continue from where it stopped.
remediation-project-inconsistent = Compare the registry with the project directory yourself. sbxm never rewrites one to match the other.
remediation-project-not-managed = Register the project and build it.
remediation-sandbox-not-created = Open the project so the first run builds its sandbox.
remediation-sandbox-not-running = Start the sandbox, then run this command again.
remediation-sandbox-workspace-missing = The workspace is an empty mount point on the host, and the repository lives inside the sandbox, so the directory holds nothing of the project. Repairing the project creates the directory and reports that it did. Creating the directory yourself works too.
remediation-sbx-login = Finish signing in, then run this command again.
remediation-prompt-unreadable = Run the command again from a terminal sbxm can read.
remediation-no-managed-projects = Register the first project.
remediation-diagnose-project = See the state the project is in.
remediation-run-global-status = Diagnose the host environment.
remediation-remote-ssh-unconfigured = Set up the Remote SSH integration of Docker Sandboxes on this host, then run this command again.
remediation-worktree-tracked-changes = Open the sandbox and look at what changed in that worktree. Commit and push what you want to keep, and review what you do not want before reverting it. Run this command again once the worktree is clean.
remediation-worktree-tracked-changes-host = Open the sandbox and look at what changed in that worktree. Commit what you want to keep and save it into the host repository, and review what you do not want before reverting it. Run this command again once the worktree is clean.
remediation-worktree-untracked-paths = Review the untracked paths listed above. Add, commit, and push what you want to keep; move or remove what you do not, after checking its contents yourself.
remediation-worktree-untracked-paths-host = Review the untracked paths listed above. Add and commit what you want to keep, then save it into the host repository; move or remove what you do not, after checking its contents yourself.
remediation-git-operation-in-progress = Look at what that worktree reports and follow its guidance to continue or abort the operation. Commit and push anything you want to keep, then run this command again once no Git operation is in progress.
remediation-git-operation-in-progress-host = Look at what that worktree reports and follow its guidance to continue or abort the operation. Commit anything you want to keep and save it into the host repository, then run this command again once no Git operation is in progress.
remediation-origin-commit-unreachable = Push a branch that reaches this commit to the origin, then run this command again once the commit is reachable from there.
remediation-host-ref-unsaveable = Saving into the host repository keeps branches, tags, and each worktree's HEAD, not this ref. Open the sandbox, put the commits you want to keep on a branch and save them, or remove the ref after checking it, then run this command again.
remediation-host-commit-unreachable = Sync the sandbox with the host repository, then run this command again. A commit that reached the host is kept even after the sandbox is removed.
remediation-origin-missing = Restore the origin configuration for this project, then run this command again. The remote URL is not shown here.
remediation-origin-refresh-failed = Fix the network or credential problem that kept the origin from refreshing, then run this command again once the origin can be reached.
remediation-origin-advertisement-invalid = Check that the origin and this build of sbxm agree on the Git protocol, then run this command again once the origin's ref list can be read.
remediation-origin-object-missing = Recover the missing commit from the origin or a backup, for example with a read-only integrity check, then run this command again once it is present.
remediation-origin-observation-unobservable = Open the sandbox and repair whatever keeps its origin from being reached, then run this command again.
remediation-origin-read-only-data-insufficient = Open the sandbox, refresh or repair its repository, and run this command again. Status never fetches or recommends a destructive force option.
remediation-origin-host-repository-unreadable = Check that the host repository is still at the path the project was added with, then run this command again.
remediation-worktree-inventory-unobservable = Check the project's status to see the worktree listing that could not be read. Repair the repository or sandbox, then run this command again once the listing succeeds.
remediation-worktree-status-unobservable = Open the sandbox, repair whatever keeps that worktree's status from being read, save or organize its contents as needed, then run this command again.
remediation-git-operation-unobservable = Open the sandbox and repair whatever keeps that worktree's Git directory from being read, then run this command again once no Git operation is in progress.
remediation-local-refs-unobservable = Repair whatever keeps the repository's branches and remotes from being read, then run this command again once they can be listed.
remediation-ignored-paths-unobservable = Open the sandbox and repair whatever keeps that worktree's ignored paths from being listed, then run this command again once they can be listed.
remediation-reflog-unobservable = Open the sandbox and repair whatever keeps that worktree's reflog from being read, then run this command again once it can be read.
remediation-remote-configuration-unobservable = Open the sandbox and repair whatever keeps that worktree's remotes and branch upstream settings from being read, then run this command again once they can be listed.
remediation-protection-not-confirmed = Run this command again and type the project ID the prompt shows.
remediation-protection-state-changed = Look at what changed, then run this command again to confirm the current state.
remediation-worktrees-not-reducible = Removing a worktree deletes whatever is checked out in it, so a re-run does not do it as a side effect. Destroy the project when that is what you want.
remediation-worktree-outside-repository = Inspect that path yourself. sbxm never deletes a worktree it cannot account for.
remediation-unmanaged-worktree-present = Save the work in that worktree, remove it from inside the sandbox, then run the rebuild again.
remediation-rebuild-generation-missing = Restore the Dockerfile of that generation and run the rebuild again.
remediation-run-rebuild = Finish the rebuild.
remediation-run-repair = Review the repair plan and run the explicit repair command again.
remediation-run-open = Resolve the reported cause, then run open again to continue and connect.
remediation-initial-provisioning-generation-missing = Restore one unambiguous target generation or inspect the project manually; sbxm will not guess or overwrite artifacts.
remediation-initial-provisioning-baseline-ambiguous = Use apply to place newly declared files explicitly, or inspect the sandbox manually. repair will not guess which declarations belonged to the original build.
remediation-initial-provisioning-unobservable = Open the project to start it and connect. sbxm does not start a sandbox only to look inside it.
remediation-target-configuration-mismatch = Leave those options out to continue with the stored target, or destroy the project first to build it differently.
remediation-image-collision = Inspect { $image } yourself, then remove or rename it once you are sure it is not needed. sbxm does not overwrite an image it did not build.
remediation-cleanup-failed = Remove { $path } yourself once you are sure nothing else needs it, then run the command again.
remediation-remove-temp-file = Inspect { $path }, then delete it once you are sure no other run is using it.
remediation-registry-entry-mismatch = Compare the registry with the project directory yourself. sbxm never rewrites a registration to make a run succeed.
remediation-project-path-occupied = Inspect { $path } yourself, then move it aside or register the project from another parent directory. sbxm never adopts artifacts it did not register.
remediation-project-path-collision = Run the command again from another parent directory. sbxm never adds an owner directory to work around a name that is already taken.
remediation-git-identity-undecidable = Register once from a terminal to choose the identity, or declare both options for this run. sbxm does not take the host's settings as your intent.
remediation-invalid-clone-url = Open the repository on GitHub, copy the clone URL it shows, and pass it unchanged.
remediation-file-declaration-not-removable = Remove the files entry for { $destination } from { $path } yourself. sbxm does not rewrite a configuration unless it can confirm that the rest stays as written.
remediation-file-not-declared = List the declared files to find the destination to remove.
remediation-file-already-declared = Remove that declaration first if this file should replace it.
remediation-file-destination-required = Name where the file goes inside the sandbox home.
remediation-config-not-rewritable = Add `{ $declaration }` to { $path } yourself, then run the command again. sbxm does not rewrite a configuration unless it can confirm that the rest stays as written.
remediation-fix-config = Edit { $path } and run the command again.
remediation-install-command = Install { $program } and make sure it is on PATH.
remediation-start-docker = Start Docker Desktop and wait until the engine reports it is running.
remediation-network-policy = Set the Docker Sandboxes network policy to { $expected } before continuing.
remediation-wait-for-lock = Wait for the other sbxm run to finish, then run the command again.
remediation-open-session-active = Wait for the remote session to end, then run the command again. Deleting the lease file by hand does not end the session.
remediation-external-command-not-confirmed = Look at what the command printed below, make sure nothing else is currently attached to the target, and run the command again.
security-config-permission-description = { $path } has mode { $observed }, which grants access beyond the owner. sbxm refuses to use a configuration that other accounts on this machine can read or change.
security-config-permission-remediation = Confirm that you own { $path }, then take the access away from every other account by restricting its mode to { $expected }.

security-config-symlink-description = { $path } is a symbolic link. Following it could read or overwrite a file outside the sbxm configuration directory.
security-config-symlink-remediation = Replace { $path } with a regular file that you own, or move the real configuration back to that path.

security-config-owner-description = { $path } belongs to user ID { $observed }, and you are user ID { $expected }. A configuration another account owns can be replaced under sbxm at any moment.
security-config-owner-remediation = Move { $path } out of the way and let sbxm create it again, or restore a file you own at that path.

security-config-dir-permission-description = { $path } has mode { $observed }, which grants access beyond the owner. Locks and configuration stored there could be observed or replaced.
security-config-dir-permission-remediation = Confirm that you own { $path }, then take the access away from every other account by restricting its mode to { $expected }.

security-config-dir-symlink-description = { $path } is a symbolic link. sbxm would create locks and configuration outside the intended directory.
security-config-dir-symlink-remediation = Replace { $path } with a directory that you own.

security-config-dir-owner-description = { $path } belongs to user ID { $observed }, and you are user ID { $expected }. Locks and configuration placed there would sit in a directory another account controls.
security-config-dir-owner-remediation = Move { $path } out of the way and let sbxm create it again, or restore a directory you own at that path.

security-project-path-symlink-description = { $path } is a symbolic link. sbxm does not follow it, because creating or replacing files through it would act on a location outside the project directory.
security-project-path-symlink-remediation = Replace { $path } with a regular file or directory that you own, then run the command again.

security-project-file-permission-description = { $path } has mode { $observed }, which grants access beyond the owner. sbxm refuses to use a file that other accounts on this machine can read or change.
security-project-file-permission-remediation = Confirm that you own { $path }, then take the access away from every other account by restricting its mode to { $expected }.

security-project-path-owner-description = { $path } belongs to user ID { $observed }, and you are user ID { $expected }. sbxm does not build a project on a path another account owns, whatever its mode says.
security-project-path-owner-remediation = Move { $path } out of the way and let sbxm create it again, or restore a path you own there.

security-ssh-agent-exposed-description = The host SSH agent can be reached from { $sandbox } ({ $observed }). An agent inside the sandbox can sign with your keys.
security-ssh-agent-exposed-remediation = Docker Sandboxes forwards the host SSH agent into every sandbox by default. Turn forwarding off in the Docker Sandboxes settings, restart its daemon so existing sandboxes pick up the change, then run the same command again. Do not remove the sandbox: that also removes its scoped secret, and the recreated sandbox would receive the agent again.


